Building Debian and Ubuntu packages
==================================

The packaging targets Debian stable and Ubuntu LTS, using native builds in a
clean sbuild/pbuilder environment for each distribution and architecture. An
Ubuntu binary package must not be reused as a Debian binary package. A Launchpad
project is not required; an account/team PPA is an optional Ubuntu build and
distribution service. Debian needs its own builder and archive.

Dependency availability
-----------------------

The qore-packagers team's testing PPA is
https://launchpad.net/~qore-packagers/+archive/ubuntu/qore-testing.
Pinned Ubuntu 26.04 dependency source recipes, the c-ares patch maintenance
procedure, and local build/sign/upload instructions are in debian/backports/README.

Qore 3.0 currently needs dependencies newer than those in the standard stable/LTS
archives. The Build-Depends versions deliberately match the upstream CMake
requirements. Before building, provide distribution-specific backports of:

  c-ares >= 1.34.8
  nghttp2 >= 1.70.0
  ngtcp2 and its matching OpenSSL crypto backend >= 1.25.0
  nghttp3 >= 1.18.0
  tree-sitter >= 0.26.13

The OpenSSL backend also needs OpenSSL >= 3.5. LLVM >= 18, Eigen >= 3.4,
ONNX Runtime >= 1.23.2, Arrow/Parquet, and the other development packages in
debian/control must be available for the target release. In particular, older
Ubuntu LTS releases need more backports than the newest LTS. Do not replace the
distribution's OpenSSL packages casually: older LTS support needs a separately
reviewed dependency backport strategy and clean installation/upgrade testing.

Normal upstream CMake builds retain FetchContent fallbacks. Debian package
builds instead set QORE_REQUIRE_SYSTEM_DEPENDENCIES=ON and
FETCHCONTENT_FULLY_DISCONNECTED=ON: required debian/rules targets must not access
the network (Debian Policy 4.9). Installing libraries manually in /usr/local is
not a substitute for packaged Build-Depends and will be ignored.

Package builds also set QORE_BUILD_WITH_EXTERNAL_MODULES=OFF and isolate Qore's
module search paths. Otherwise an optional module installed on the builder
(for example XML) can become an undeclared hard dependency of the AOT-compiled
standard library. The option covers compilation, metadata extraction, and
documentation; ordinary upstream builds keep optional module discovery enabled.

If a distributor chooses to bundle fallback sources instead of supplying
backports, those sources must be included in the source package before upload,
with checksums, their full license notices, and all transitive build inputs.
Point FETCHCONTENT_SOURCE_DIR_<NAME> at those unpacked sources, adjust the
Build-Depends and QORE_REQUIRE_SYSTEM_DEPENDENCIES setting for that derivative,
and verify the whole build without network access. FetchContent's download
cache on the maintainer's machine is not part of a source package. This tree
does not supply such a bundled dependency source package.

The source package is not ready for an unmodified stock stable/LTS builder until
the dependency gap above is resolved. Do not bypass dpkg-checkbuilddeps to claim
distribution compatibility.

Source and binary builds
------------------------

The source format is 3.0 (quilt), matching the upstream-version plus Debian
revision in debian/changelog. Supply the upstream release tarball as
../qore_<upstream-version>.orig.tar.xz (or .gz). For a Git snapshot, first commit
the intended upstream changes and create a clean upstream archive, for example:

  git archive --format=tar --prefix=qore-3.0.0/ HEAD -- . ':!debian' \
      | xz > ../qore_3.0.0.orig.tar.xz
  dpkg-buildpackage -S -us -uc

Build the resulting .dsc in a clean builder with the required backport archive
configured. For local binary testing in an already provisioned environment:

  dpkg-checkbuilddeps
  dpkg-buildpackage -b -us -uc

Use a release-specific changelog distribution and a distinct version for each
Ubuntu series/PPA upload. Only source packages are uploaded to a PPA. Dependency
backports must be published before the Qore build can satisfy Build-Depends.

Build options
-------------

DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS supports parallel=N, nocheck, noopt, nostrip, and nodoc.
The nodoc build profile also omits qore-doc and its documentation dependencies.
Architecture-only builds (-B) do not generate documentation. Architecture-
independent builds (-A) still build the native tools needed to generate docs.
Cross builds are rejected because the build executes qpp, qore, and qcc.

The CMake build type is None so dpkg-buildflags controls optimization and
hardening, including noopt. Installed CMake-built ELF objects omit RUNPATH;
chrpath removes qcc's build-directory RUNPATH from AOT modules before stripping.
The dh_strip wrapper preserves Qore's appended dependency, source-location and
optional-module metadata while keeping normal stripping and dbgsym generation.
Without the optional-module metadata, installing an external module could leave
the compiled standard library silently using the branch for its absence.
The source tree is not populated with generated qmod symlinks. Optional MongoDB,
OCR, and Linux io_uring dependencies are declared to keep package contents stable
between clean builders. ONNX Runtime is required for the packaged ml module;
QORE_REQUIRE_ONNXRUNTIME=ON prevents a build from silently omitting inference
support. Ubuntu 26.04 provides libonnxruntime-dev; releases without a suitable
package need a backport. Runtime dependencies are generated from the linked
library. CPU inference is included; GPU execution providers require separate
platform-specific qualification.

Package boundaries and ABI
--------------------------

libqore20 contains only the SONAME library and is Multi-Arch: same. Debhelper
generates its ldconfig trigger and versioned shlibs dependency; there is no
hand-maintained minimum version that can become stale as the C++ API grows.
Changing the SONAME requires renaming this package and updating its dependents.
Before publishing 3.0, compare the exported C++ ABI with the previously released
libqore20; an incompatible change requires a SONAME/package-name bump. Qore's
binary module API version is separate from the shared-library SONAME.

qore-stdlib is Architecture: any: AOT .qmod files are ELF objects installed under
the multiarch libdir, while Qore sources/resources live under /usr/share. It is
not marked Multi-Arch: same; shared data and AOT output have not been qualified
for simultaneous installations of multiple architectures.

libqore-dev contains qcc, qpp, CMake/pkg-config integration, the compiler format
stamp, AOT linker configuration, and the API metadata generator. It depends on
the interpreter, C++ compiler, public OpenSSL headers, and the LLVM development
package matching the build, including the public LLVM plugin API headers.

YAML and all twelve companion user modules ship in qore-stdlib. Its versioned
Provides and Breaks/Replaces replace pre-3.0 qore-yaml-module packages.
Remote debugger clients live in qore-debug-tools and use the bundled YAML
module. qore itself includes the local debugger. Package moves include
Breaks/Replaces for upgrades from pre-3.0 packages. Architecture-independent
tools accept binary-only rebuilds of qore instead of requiring source:Version
to equal binary:Version.

qore-doc contains generated HTML and examples. Its docs remain under its own
package directory; they are not silently omitted or installed into the live
system by the upstream install-docs target.

Validation before publishing
----------------------------

  lintian ../qore_*.changes
  autopkgtest ../qore_*.dsc ../*.deb -- <testbed backend and arguments>

Run the installed-package tests in a clean testbed with the checkout excluded
from module search paths. They cover native and AOT module loading, interpreter
execution modes, C++ embedding via pkg-config and CMake, standalone AOT output,
source-free compiled module loading, and CLI startup. Test both default and
nodoc/architecture-only builds, installation with Recommends disabled, upgrades
from the previously published packages, removal/purge, and rebuild
reproducibility before publishing a release. These are release qualification
steps; adding the tests alone is not evidence that each release passed them.
